# Architecture

## Objective

Keep changes consistent with the project's existing architectural boundaries, layering, and patterns.

## Core Principles

- Identify the existing architectural pattern (MVC, layered, hexagonal, feature-based, etc.) before adding code.
- New code goes in the layer/module that already owns that responsibility.
- Don't introduce a second way to do something the project already does one way (e.g. a second HTTP client, a second state pattern).
- Cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, error handling) go through existing shared mechanisms, not new ad-hoc ones.

## Requirements

- Before adding a module, check whether an existing one already owns that responsibility.
- Keep dependency direction consistent with the current layering (e.g. UI never imports directly from the DB layer if that's not the existing pattern).
- If the architecture genuinely needs to change, say so explicitly and explain the trade-off — don't do it silently.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Adding a new framework/library to solve something an existing dependency already solves.
- Bypassing an existing service/repository layer to query the database directly 'just this once'.
- Creating parallel folder structures that duplicate an existing convention.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ The change fits the existing layering without new, unexplained shortcuts.
✓ No duplicate mechanisms were introduced for something the project already handles.
✓ Any architectural deviation was called out explicitly with a reason.

## Golden Rule

Consistency with existing architecture beats local optimization almost every time.
